  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The best solution is to use a mapping template on the <b>Integration Request</b> to filter out obsolete fields from the incoming JSON requests, ensuring that only valid fields are forwarded to the Lambda backend. This approach is correct because it mediates the request between the clients (who may still be sending old data) and the upgraded Lambda (which expects the new format), effectively allowing both old and new clients to coexist until migration is complete.</p><ul><li><i>Set up a canary deployment stage in the original API to monitor ongoing use of the outdated JSON fields before deprecating the service.</i>: This option does not solve the problem—it merely tracks usage and doesn't prevent outdated data from reaching the Lambda.</li><li><b>Apply a mapping template to the Integration Request to strip away deprecated fields from incoming JSON, ensuring only current values are sent to the Lambda function.</b>: This is the correct approach because the Integration Request mapping template can modify the body of the requests before they reach the backend, allowing seamless filtering of legacy fields.</li><li><i>Integrate a new Lambda function as an authorizer in the original API so only well-formatted JSON requests are permitted to reach the backend.</i>: Lambda authorizers are typically used for authentication and authorization, not for filtering request data or transforming payloads.</li><li><i>Configure a mapping template within the Integration Response to filter obsolete fields from the Lambda's output before returning a response to clients.</i>: The Integration Response mapping only affects the response that goes back to the client after Lambda has already processed the request, so this does not help filter fields out before reaching Lambda.</li></ul><p><b>Tips:</b> Use Integration Request mapping templates to modify inputs to Lambda, and Integration Response mapping templates to alter outputs to the client. Always transform data as close to the source as possible to ensure backend compatibility.</p>",
  "query": "<p>An AWS API Gateway endpoint with a Lambda backend has been processing requests for several years. Some of the fields in the incoming JSON body are now outdated and must no longer be stored. The Lambda function has been updated to only accept the new valid JSON fields. However, since the existing API endpoint continues to serve users during the transition to a new API, how can the current API Gateway be adjusted so that the Lambda receives only the updated, accepted JSON data, even from older client requests?</p>",
